n?", "choice_a": "GIP and GLP-1 receptors", "choice_b": "GLP-1 and opioid mu receptors", "choice_c": "Amylin and nicotinic receptors", "choice_d": "GIP and serotonin 5-HT3 receptors", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Tirzepatide is a dual glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide and GLP-1 receptor agonist.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [1], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "What is the most accurate interpretation of Elkin et al.'s pooled aspiration result, OR 1.04 (95% CI 0.87\u20131.25)?", "choice_a": "Aspiration was proven impossible in GLP-1 users", "choice_b": "No statistically significant association was demonstrated, with low-certainty evidence", "choice_c": "GLP-1 therapy caused a fourfold increase in aspiration", "choice_d": "All included studies were randomized trials establishing equivalence", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The confidence interval includes 1, and the evidence was observational and low certainty. This does not establish zero risk or equivalence.", "source_ids": [4], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40230298/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "A qualified gastric ultrasound examination estimates clear gastric fluid at 2 mL/kg. What is the best interpretation?", "choice_a": "It proves that 2 mL/kg will be aspirated during induction", "choice_b": "It is automatically normal because the fluid is clear", "choice_c": "It eliminates the need to consider procedure urgency", "choice_d": "It exceeds the commonly used greater-than-1.5 mL/kg high-RGC study threshold and should inform the clinical plan", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "An estimate above 1.5 mL/kg meets a commonly used high-RGC definition. Ultrasound findings inform risk assessment; they do not predict an exact aspirated volume.", "source_ids": [2, 5, 6], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41032460/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42265610/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "A stable patient takes oral semaglutide daily, has no significant GI symptoms, and follows the facility's SPAQI preparation pathway. What does SPAQI recommend regarding the GLP-1 medication?", "choice_a": "Routinely hold for four weeks because it is oral", "choice_b": "Continue perioperatively, with the pathway's specified diet and fasting instructions", "choice_c": "Always omit the morning dose as SPAQI's standard rule", "choice_d": "Replace it with pramlintide while fasting", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "SPAQI recommends continuation in patients without significant GI symptoms. Oral daily dosing does not imply a short half-life; oral semaglutide has approximately a one-week half-life.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 2], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which approach matches the 2025 ADS/ANZCA/GESA/NACOS recommendations?", "choice_a": "Stop every weekly agent for a month regardless of symptoms", "choice_b": "Continue therapy with a 24-hour clear-fluid diet followed by the document's standard fasting pathway", "choice_c": "Use a full-liquid diet until two hours before anesthesia", "choice_d": "Ignore dietary preparation when the patient has no nausea", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The Australian/New Zealand recommendations support continuation with a 24-hour clear-fluid diet followed by the stated standard six-hour fast. Apply that guidance in its local protocol context; do not combine it with unrelated fasting rules.", "source_ids": [3], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420935/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "What conclusion is supported by RESIDUAL's 42% versus 24% increased-RGC findings after at least a seven-day hold?", "choice_a": "A seven-day hold guarantees an empty stomach", "choice_b": "The study proved a statistically significant doubling of aspiration", "choice_c": "Retained contents can persist after a seven-day hold, but this underpowered comparison was statistically inconclusive", "choice_d": "All patients require a hold longer than 21 days", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "RESIDUAL measured gastric contents, not a proven difference in aspiration. Its confidence intervals included no difference and the study was underpowered.", "source_ids": [6], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42265610/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Why should residual gastric content and pulmonary aspiration be reported separately?", "choice_a": "Retained contents are a surrogate risk marker; aspiration is a distinct clinical event", "choice_b": "Every patient with retained fluid will aspirate", "choice_c": "An increased-RGC odds ratio directly equals the absolute aspiration probability", "choice_d": "They are interchangeable names for the same outcome", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The meta-analysis found increased residual contents but did not demonstrate increased aspiration with low-certainty evidence. The outcomes cannot be treated as synonymous.", "source_ids": [4], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40230298/"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A patient has recurrent vomiting and cannot tolerate oral intake before an elective procedure. Under SPAQI, what is the appropriate next step?", "choice_a": "Proceed solely because one weekly dose was missed", "choice_b": "Permit a protein shake immediately before anesthesia", "choice_c": "Treat the symptoms as irrelevant if the patient denies reflux", "choice_d": "Refer for prescribing-team diet/medication modification and reassessment before elective anesthesia", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Severe nausea, vomiting, and inability to tolerate intake are significant symptoms in SPAQI's framework. They warrant modification and reassessment rather than reassurance from a fixed hold.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which statement best describes a reassuring gastric ultrasound assessment?", "choice_a": "One apparently empty antral image proves a completely empty stomach", "choice_b": "An inconclusive image should be recorded as negative", "choice_c": "A technically adequate examination interpreted in context can reduce uncertainty but does not guarantee zero aspiration risk", "choice_d": "A reassuring scan removes all other airway considerations", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Gastric ultrasound has operator, technical, and patient-related limitations. A limited or single-view examination should not be overinterpreted.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A patient is fasting for surgery and normally uses pramlintide with mealtime insulin. Which instruction matches its Medication Guide?", "choice_a": "Do not inject pramlintide when unable to eat or skipping the meal; maintain a separate individualized insulin plan", "choice_b": "Continue pramlintide while fasting because all GLP-1 agents should continue", "choice_c": "Use pramlintide instead of necessary basal insulin", "choice_d": "Double pramlintide to offset surgical stress", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Pramlintide is an amylin analog with meal-dependent dosing, not a GLP-1 agent. It should not be injected when the patient cannot eat, and it does not replace insulin.", "source_ids": [8], "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=4aea30ff-eb0d-45c1-b114-3127966328ff"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Under SPAQI, what solid-food restriction is recommended for a GLP-1 patient without significant GI symptoms?", "choice_a": "No solids for exactly 21.3 hours as a validated safe threshold", "choice_b": "A full-liquid diet without restrictions until induction", "choice_c": "No solids for 24 hours, with clear liquids only during that interval and the specified liquid-fasting cutoffs", "choice_d": "The same two-hour interval for solids and water", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "SPAQI specifies a 24-hour solid fast with clear liquids only, followed by its separate 8-hour or 4-hour liquid intervals. A ROC threshold is not a replacement protocol.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which entry best supports clinical continuity after an individualized GLP-1 decision?", "choice_a": "Only weekly versus daily dosing, without symptoms or intake times", "choice_b": "The medication and preparation history, risk assessment, chosen mitigation, rationale, and recovery/restart plan", "choice_c": "Only antiemetic orders, without the aspiration-risk assessment", "choice_d": "Only the last-dose date, without the rationale or recovery plan", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Clinically useful documentation communicates what was assessed, why the plan was chosen, and what the next team needs to know. A single isolated medication detail is not a complete clinical handoff.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "What does the November 2024 Ozempic aspiration warning emphasize?", "choice_a": "Rare aspiration has been reported during general anesthesia or deep sedation, and patients should disclose use to procedural clinicians", "choice_b": "Aspiration is impossible after standard fasting", "choice_c": "A one-day hold is proven to prevent retained contents", "choice_d": "Only patients using the drug for diabetes require assessment", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The label warns about reported aspiration and notes insufficient data to establish whether modified fasting or temporary interruption reduces retained contents.", "source_ids": [7], "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/209637s032lbl.pdf"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 3], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A GLP-1 patient also uses high-dose opioids and has known gastroparesis. What is the best approach?", "choice_a": "Individualize beyond the routine pathway because additional causes of delayed emptying are present", "choice_b": "Assume SPAQI's routine fasting table guarantees safety", "choice_c": "Ignore the opioids if the GLP-1 drug was held for a week", "choice_d": "Use absence of nausea as proof of normal emptying", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "SPAQI's routine recommendations do not substitute for judgment in patients with other important causes of delayed gastric emptying, including gastroparesis or high-dose opioid use.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "When dexamethasone and ondansetron are selected for adult PONV prophylaxis, which timing is appropriate?", "choice_a": "Both drugs must always be given only after vomiting begins", "choice_b": "Ondansetron at induction and dexamethasone only at discharge", "choice_c": "All GLP-1 patients require three prophylactic agents at induction", "choice_d": "Dexamethasone at induction and ondansetron near the end of surgery", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The cited current PONV summary lists dexamethasone 4\u20138 mg IV at induction and ondansetron 4 mg IV at the end of surgery. Individualize the regimen and contraindications.", "source_ids": [9], "source_urls": ["https://dig.pharmacy.uic.edu/faqs/2026-2/may-2026-faqs/what-are-current-recommendations-for-prevention-and-treatment-of-post-operative-nausea-and-vomiting-ponv/"], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which finding meets the high-RGC definition used in the cited ultrasound studies?", "choice_a": "Any visible trace of clear gastric fluid", "choice_b": "Only fluid above 5 mL/kg", "choice_c": "An empty antrum on one technically limited view", "choice_d": "Solid content, thick fluid, or estimated clear fluid greater than 1.5 mL/kg", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The research classification includes solids, thick contents, or clear fluid above 1.5 mL/kg. It is not a threshold at which aspiration is certain.", "source_ids": [5, 6], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41032460/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42265610/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Under 2024 multisociety guidance, a stable daily-liraglutide patient has no elevated delayed-emptying risk. What is reasonable?", "choice_a": "A mandatory four-week interruption", "choice_b": "Routine cancellation because the patient took today's dose", "choice_c": "Continuation, using the institutional preparation pathway and individualized assessment", "choice_d": "An automatic rapid-sequence induction based only on daily dosing", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The multisociety guidance permits continuation when elevated risk is absent. The legacy hold schedule is a fallback when shared decision-making specifically favors withholding.", "source_ids": [2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Under SPAQI, which liquid-fasting intervals are specified for a GLP-1 patient without significant symptoms?", "choice_a": "2 hours for all liquids, including protein shakes", "choice_b": "4 hours for high-carbohydrate clear liquids and 8 hours for water", "choice_c": "8 hours for clear liquids containing \u226510% glucose and 4 hours for those containing <10%", "choice_d": "24 hours with no fluid intake for every patient", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The specific SPAQI recommendations distinguish high-carbohydrate from no-/low-carbohydrate clear liquids. They do not allow non-clear liquids to be treated as water.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "A time-sensitive case requires general anesthesia and gastric ultrasound shows retained solids. What is the most appropriate planning principle?", "choice_a": "Use deep sedation without an airway rescue plan", "choice_b": "Discuss proceeding versus delay and, if proceeding, use appropriate full-stomach airway management with difficult-airway contingencies", "choice_c": "Ignore the solids because seven days have elapsed since the drug dose", "choice_d": "Administer ondansetron as the sole aspiration-prevention measure", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Confirmed retained contents require a risk-benefit discussion. Multisociety guidance includes considering rapid-sequence induction and tracheal intubation versus cancellation; airway difficulty and urgency still matter.", "source_ids": [2, 3], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420935/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which statement about GLP-1 therapy and perioperative glucose is most accurate?", "choice_a": "GLP-1 drugs stimulate insulin independently of glucose in every patient", "choice_b": "Continuing or stopping therapy never affects perioperative glycemia", "choice_c": "Glucose monitoring is unnecessary during reduced intake", "choice_d": "Glucose-dependent insulin secretion does not remove hypoglycemia risk from concomitant insulin or sulfonylureas, and withholding may worsen glycemia", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Assess the whole medication and nutrition plan. The label notes greater hypoglycemia risk with insulin or insulin secretagogues, and guidance warns about metabolic consequences of withholding.", "source_ids": [1, 2, 7], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/209637s032lbl.pdf"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A patient increased a long-acting GLP-1 dose two weeks ago. What does this mean for assessment?", "choice_a": "It is irrelevant if the prescription is for weight management", "choice_b": "It guarantees that aspiration will occur", "choice_c": "It automatically establishes that the stomach is empty", "choice_d": "Escalation is a higher-risk feature that should prompt symptom, intake, preparation, and timing review", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Escalation can increase delayed-emptying and GI effects. It warrants individualized precautions or deferral when appropriate, not a prediction of inevitable aspiration.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "When is postoperative resumption of a GLP-1 receptor agonist generally appropriate under SPAQI?", "choice_a": "Immediately after extubation even with persistent vomiting", "choice_b": "Once the usual diet is resumed, with symptoms and any product-specific restart or re-titration needs addressed", "choice_c": "Only after doubling the next dose", "choice_d": "Never again after any anesthetic", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "SPAQI ties resumption to the usual diet. Significant ongoing symptoms and a prolonged interruption require review; product and prescriber instructions govern missed-dose or re-titration decisions.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which principle best summarizes perioperative GLP-1 care?", "choice_a": "Use a named guidance pathway with individualized risk assessment, shared decisions, appropriate precautions, and a documented restart plan", "choice_b": "Apply one fixed hold interval without considering symptoms or procedure", "choice_c": "Treat a normal single ultrasound image as a guarantee", "choice_d": "Replace clinical assessment with memorization of ROC cutoffs", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The guidance emphasizes balancing metabolic needs and aspiration concerns. Diet, assessment, anesthetic technique, and follow-up must fit the patient rather than an unvalidated universal interval.", "source_ids": [1, 2, 3], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420935/"], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}]''',
);
